6455078125, 0.037689208984375, 0.0174713134765625, 0.016448974609375, -0.00467681884765625, -0.020965576171875, -0.02398681640625, 0.01116180419921875, -0.0161590576171875, 0.0012111663818359375, 0.048858642578125, 0.033599853515625, -0.01374053955078125, 0.00756072998046875, 0.00815582275390625, 0.0157470703125, 0.031768798828125, -0.032867431640625, -0.0220794677734375, 0.021697998046875, 0.0166168212890625, 0.00972747802734375, -0.0117034912109375, 0.012908935546875, -0.02142333984375, 0.0004112720489501953, 0.03424072265625, 0.004608154296875, -0.0017099380493164062, 0.0251617431640625, -0.0203857421875, 0.024322509765625, -0.004474639892578125, 0.0014801025390625, -0.004718780517578125, 0.00572967529296875, 0.0187530517578125, 0.0207672119140625, -0.005245208740234375, 0.01397705078125, 0.0298309326171875, -0.008270263671875, 0.01904296875, -0.021148681640625, 0.019439697265625, 0.0296630859375, -0.0418701171875, 0.006870269775390625, -0.01480865478515625, 0.0204620361328125, 0.0196685791015625, -0.033599853515625, 0.01416778564453125, 0.004749298095703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and complex meshed (cyclic) topologies. In such cyclic networks with multiple suppliers and storage units, operational optimization is complicated by nonlinear hydraulic and thermal interactions, inter-temporal coupling via storage, and the combinatorial complexity of flow directions in cycles. Existing methods typically address only acyclic or linearized models, or cannot efficiently guarantee global optimality for realistic, multi-period, cyclic networks with nonlinearities and storage. For example, in a network with two suppliers and a storage tank connected in a loop, determining the optimal flow and temperature profiles over time requires resolving nonlinear pressure drops (which relate flow to pressure loss in pipes), mixing of water at network junctions (which determines outgoing temperatures), and storage dynamics (which couple decisions across time), all while avoiding non-physical cyclic flows\u2014flows that circulate endlessly in a loop without serving any demand. Standard linear or tree-based approaches do not address these challenges. To our knowledge, no prior work provides a global optimization framework for nonlinear, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, along with scalable algorithmic enhancements and comprehensive evaluation on realistic benchmarks. This work fills that gap.\n\n---\n\n**Physical Model and Key Assumptions**  \nWe model the district heating network as a directed graph \\( G = (V, E) \\), where nodes \\( V \\) represent junctions, suppliers, consumers, or storage units, and edges \\( E \\) represent pipes. The following physical assumptions are made for tractability and clarity:\n\n- **Perfect Mixing:** Water at each node is assumed to mix instantaneously and perfectly, so outgoing temperature is a flow-weighted average of incoming streams.\n- **Neglect of Heat Losses:** Heat losses along pipes are neglected; all pipes are assumed perfectly insulated. This is justified for short time horizons, well-insulated networks, or when heat losses are small relative to total flows. In networks with significant heat losses or over long horizons, this assumption may lead to optimistic estimates of efficiency; extensions to include heat losses are possible but not considered here.\n- **Constant Fluid Properties:** Water density and specific heat are assumed constant and independent of temperature and pressure, which is standard for moderate temperature ranges in district heating.\n- **No Minimum Flow Thresholds:** Minimum flow rates in pipes are neglected, assuming that operational flows always exceed practical thresholds. In practice, if flows approach zero, additional constraints may be needed to ensure physical feasibility.\n- **No Pipe Capacity Limits:** Unless otherwise stated, pipe flow rates are assumed unbounded for modeling simplicity; practical limits can be added as linear constraints. This assumption is reasonable for preliminary design or when pipe sizing is not a limiting factor, but may overestimate feasible flows in highly constrained networks.\n- **No Pressure-Driven Demand:** Consumer demands are assumed fixed and independent of network pressure, which is appropriate when consumer-side controls maintain demand regardless of supply pressure.\n\nThese assumptions are standard in the literature for first-principles modeling and allow focus on the core nonlinear and combinatorial challenges. Their impact on solution validity is discussed in the limitations section; in scenarios where these assumptions do not hold, model extensions are possible.\n\n---\n\n**Main Contributions**  \nTo address the above challenges, this work makes the following contributions:\n\n- **Global Optimization Framework:** We develop a global optimization framework for cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, capturing nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints under the above physical assumptions.\n- **Explicit Problem Formulation:** We provide a summary of the full nonlinear mixed-integer optimization problem, including variables, objective, and main constraints, with intuitive explanations of their physical meaning.\n- **Algorithmic Enhancements:** We propose five novel algorithmic enhancements to accelerate spatial branch-and-bound for these networks. These include: (1) cycle-breaking constraints to eliminate non-physical flows, (2) symmetry-breaking constraints to reduce redundant search, (3) strengthened relaxations for nonlinear pressure drops, (4) valid inequalities for storage operation, and (5) adaptive branching strategies based on network topology. Each enhancement is formulated mathematically and illustrated with examples; key steps and decision criteria are summarized below, with full pseudocode and technical details in the supplementary material.\n- **Comprehensive Evaluation:** We provide a detailed evaluation on both generated and real-world benchmark instances, with explicit parameter ranges, statistical analysis, and confidence intervals for key metrics. Main evaluation metrics and findings are summarized below.\n- **Time Decomposition Analysis:** We analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with storage, rigorously discussing its assumptions, empirical validity, and limitations.\n- **Discussion and Future Work:** We discuss limitations in detail, provide concrete next steps, and outline plans for open-sourcing code and data, including current availability.\n\n---\n\n**Notation and Symbol Table**  \nFor clarity, we define the main symbols used throughout the formulation:\n\n| Symbol | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| \\( q_{e,t} \\) | Flow rate (mass per unit time) in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (positive/negative indicates direction) |\n| \\( T_{v,t} \\) | Water temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}} \\) | Temperature of water entering pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} \\) | Outgoing temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( s_{j,t} \\) | State of charge (energy stored) for storage unit \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( x_{e,t} \\) | Binary variable indicating flow direction in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) (1 if forward, 0 if reverse) |\n| \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}} \\), \\( q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\) | Non-negative variables for energy charged/discharged from storage \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( \\Delta p_{e,t} \\) | Pressure drop across pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( k_e \\) | Pipe friction coefficient for pipe \\( e \\) |\n| \\( \\eta_{\\text{charge}} \\), \\( \\eta_{\\text{discharge}} \\) | Charging/discharging efficiency for storage units |\n| \\( T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\), \\( T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\) | Min/max allowable temperatures for supplier \\( i \\) |\n| \\( S_j^{\\max} \\) | Maximum storage capacity for storage unit \\( j \\) |\n| \\( d_{c,t} \\) | Heat demand at consumer node \\( c \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n| \\( p_{v,t} \\) | Pressure at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\) |\n\n---\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nBelow we summarize the main variables, objective, and constraints. Additional details and full constraint lists are provided in the supplementary material.\n\n**Variables and Node Types:**  \n- **Supplier nodes (\\( i \\in \\text{suppliers} \\)):** Inject heat and mass into the network; variables include supply flow \\( q_{i,t} \\) and temperature \\( T_{i,t} \\).\n- **Consumer nodes (\\( c \\in \\text{consumers} \\)):** Withdraw fixed heat demand \\( d_{c,t} \\) at each time \\( t \\).\n- **Storage nodes (\\( j \\in \\text{storage} \\)):** Store and release thermal energy; variables include state of charge \\( s_{j,t} \\), charge/discharge flows.\n- **Junction nodes:** Mix incoming flows; variables include outgoing temperature \\( T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} \\).\n\n**Objective:**  \nMinimize total operational cost over all time periods, including supplier fuel costs and storage operation costs:\n\\[\n\\min \\sum_{t} \\sum_{i \\in \\text{suppliers}} c_i(q_{i,t}, T_{i,t}) + \\sum_{j \\in \\text{storage}} c_j(s_{j,t})\n\\]\nwhere \\( c_i \\) and \\( c_j \\) are cost functions for suppliers and storage, respectively.\n\n**Main Constraints (with physical interpretation):**  \n- **Nonlinear pressure drops:**  \n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{e,t} = k_e \\cdot q_{e,t}^2 \\quad \\forall e, t\n  \\]\n  (Pressure loss in each pipe is proportional to the square of the flow, modeling pipe friction.)\n- **Pressure bounds:**  \n  \\[\n  p_{v,t}^{\\min} \\leq p_{v,t} \\leq p_{v,t}^{\\max} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n  (Ensures pressures remain within safe operating limits.)\n- **Temperature mixing at nodes:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}}}{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t}} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n  (Outgoing temperature at a node is the flow-weighted average of incoming temperatures, assuming perfect mixing.)\n- **Supply/return temperature bounds:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\leq T_{i,t} \\leq T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\quad \\forall i \\in \\text{suppliers}, t\n  \\]\n  (Ensures water temperatures remain within safe and efficient operating ranges.)\n- **Mass and energy conservation at nodes:**  \n  \\[\n  \\sum_{"}}